In `ASN1Decoder.readLength()`, the out-of-bounds check compares the decoded content `length` against the total buffer size (`limit = bytes.length`):
```java
if (length >= limit) {
    throw new IOException("corrupted stream - out of bounds length found");
}
```
But the decoder has already consumed some bytes (tag/length bytes), tracked in `count` via `read()`. This means a declared length can be larger than the *remaining* unread bytes while still being `< limit`, bypassing the check and later failing in `read(int length)` with an EOF-based error (`"EOF found reading %d bytes"`). The finding’s

